cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its measurements, please cite it as below."
title: "REFAL-SUPERSYSTEM — Valentin Turchin's four-layer Refal supersystem, implemented"
abstract: >-
  An implementation of the architecture Valentin Turchin set out in A Supersystem
  of Language Refal (1991): a Refal-5 machine (L0), a reflection engine (L1), a
  supercompiler core (L2) and a meta-prover (L3) over one shared expression space,
  with self-application (L4) as the headline capability. The Refal-5 compiler is
  written in Refal, compiles its own source, and drives rather than re-prints.
  Every capability carries a gate; the completion figure is measured, not claimed.
authors:
  - family-names: Sharma
    given-names: Abhinav
type: software
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Abhinav-Rust/REFAL-SUPERSYSTEM"
keywords:
  - Refal
  - Refal-5
  - Valentin Turchin
  - supercompilation
  - metasystem transition
  - metacomputation
  - partial evaluation
  - Futamura projections
  - program transformation
  - term rewriting
  - symbolic computation
  - self-hosting compiler
  - automated theorem proving
  - program verification
  - metaprogramming
  - homoiconic language
  - graph of states
  - residual program synthesis
  - cybernetics
  - Principia Cybernetica
references:
  - type: report
    title: "A Supersystem of Language Refal"
    authors:
      - family-names: Turchin
        given-names: Valentin F.
    year: 1991
    notes: >-
      The design this repository implements: four layers realized as specialized
      configurations of a single universal reflective engine.
  - type: report
    title: "The Concept of a Supercompiler"
    authors:
      - family-names: Turchin
        given-names: Valentin F.
    year: 1986
    notes: >-
      §6 states the proof criterion this repository implements: a predicate's
      configuration graph reduces to the single terminal node 'True'.
  - type: book
    title: "Semantics Definitions in Refal and Automatic Production of Compilers"
    authors:
      - family-names: Turchin
        given-names: Valentin F.
    year: 1980
    notes: >-
      The driving, cleaning, generalization and residualization rules (§4.2-§4.6),
      and Theorem 5.1 (§5.8) on the limits of perfection.
  - type: report
    title: "The Supercompiler SCP4: General Outline"
    authors:
      - family-names: Turchin
        given-names: Valentin F.
    year: 1999
    notes: >-
      §4 names the three theorems this repository gates: associativity of Append,
      correctness of binary tree reversals, and equality of sorting algorithms.
